    "title": "Differentiating $e^{f(x)}$ using the chain rule",
    "difficulty": 1,
    "problem": "Find $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(e^{4x - 1}\\right)$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "step": 1,
        "instruction": "Identify the inner function.",
        "working": "The outer function is $e^u$ and the inner function is $u = 4x - 1$."
      },
      {
        "step": 2,
        "instruction": "Differentiate the inner function.",
        "working": "$$\\frac{du}{dx} = 4$$"
      },
      {
        "step": 3,
        "instruction": "Apply the chain rule.",
        "working": "$$\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(e^{f(x)}\\right) = f'(x)\\,e^{f(x)}$$ so $$\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(e^{4x-1}\\right) = 4e^{4x-1}$$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(e^{4x-1}\\right) = 4e^{4x-1}$"
  },
  {
    "id": "exp-log-deriv-worked-2",
    "title": "Differentiating $\\ln f(x)$ using the chain rule",
    "difficulty": 2,
    "problem": "Find $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\ln(x^2 + 3x)\\right)$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "step": 1,
        "instruction": "Identify $f(x)$ and find $f'(x)$.",
        "working": "$$f(x) = x^2 + 3x \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad f'(x) = 2x + 3$$"
      },
      {
        "step": 2,
        "instruction": "Apply the standard result $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\ln f(x)\\right) = \\dfrac{f'(x)}{f(x)}$.",
        "working": "$$\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\ln(x^2 + 3x)\\right) = \\frac{2x + 3}{x^2 + 3x}$$ This can be factored as $\\dfrac{2x+3}{x(x+3)}$, but either form is acceptable."
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\ln(x^2 + 3x)\\right) = \\dfrac{2x + 3}{x^2 + 3x}$, equivalently $\\dfrac{2x+3}{x(x+3)}$."
  },
  {
